WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.100
This episode is about a really nice new paper
on pose estimation.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.360
Pose estimation means that we have an image
or video of a human as an input, and the output

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:19.250
should be, this skeleton that you see here
that shows us what the current position of

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.930
this person is.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:24.760
Sounds alright, but what are the applications
of this, really?

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:30.240
Well, it has a huge swath of applications,
for instance, many of you often hear about

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:36.670
motion capture for video games and animation
movies, but it is also used in medical applications

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:43.350
for finding abnormalities in a patient’s
posture, animal tracking, understanding sign

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.499
language, pedestrian detection for self-driving
cars, and much, much more.

00:00:48.499 --> 00:00:53.250
So if we can do something like this in real
time, that’s hugely beneficial for many

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.300
many applications.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:01.100
However, this is a very challenging task,
because humans have a large variety of appearances,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.890
images come in all kinds of possible viewpoints,
and as a result, the algorithm has to deal

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.710
with occlusions as well.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.720
This is particularly hard, have a look here.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.750
In these two cases, we don’t see the left
elbow, so it has to be inferred from seeing

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.710
the remainder of the body.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.660
We have the reference solution on the right,
and as you see here, this new method is significantly

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.090
closer to it than any of the previous works.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.180
Quite remarkable.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:35.610
The main idea in this paper is that it works
out the poses both in 2D and 3D and contains

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:42.390
neural network that can convert to both directions
between these representations while retaining

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.410
the consistencies between them.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:49.540
First, the technique comes up with an initial
guess, and follows up by using these pose

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:54.210
transformer networks to further refine this
initial guess.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.230
This makes all the difference.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:01.700
And not does it lead to high-quality results,
but it also takes way less time than previous

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:08.090
algorithms — we can expect to obtain a predicted
pose in about 51 milliseconds, which is almost

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.039
20 frames per second.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:14.530
This is close to real time, and is more than
enough for many of the applications we’ve

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.200
talked about earlier.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.250
In the age of rapidly improving hardware,
these are already fantastic results both in

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:26.460
terms of quality and performance, and not
only the hardware, but the papers are also

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.320
improving at a remarkable pace.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.470
What a time to be alive.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:35.910
The paper contains an exhaustive evaluation
section, it is measured against a variety

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:40.470
of high-quality solutions, I recommend that
you have a look in the video description.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:45.959
I hope nobody is going to install a system
in my lab that starts beeping every time I

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:51.060
slouch a little, but I am really looking forward
to benefitting from these other applications.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.510
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

